1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,804
Almost two thousand years ago, a Roman

2
00:00:02,804 --> 00:00:05,535
emperor wrote a strange note to himself.

3
00:00:06,185 --> 00:00:08,444
"I have often wondered how it is that

4
00:00:08,444 --> 00:00:10,868
every man loves himself more than all the

5
00:00:10,868 --> 00:00:11,750
rest of men,

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,521
and yet sets less value on his own

7
00:00:14,521 --> 00:00:16,978
opinion of himself than on the opinion of

8
00:00:16,978 --> 00:00:17,648
others."

9
00:00:18,298 --> 00:00:20,553
The man was Marcus Aurelius — the most

10
00:00:20,553 --> 00:00:22,545
powerful person in the known world.

11
00:00:22,545 --> 00:00:24,642
And even he couldn't stop caring what

12
00:00:24,642 --> 00:00:25,586
people thought.

13
00:00:26,236 --> 00:00:28,583
Today, we check how many people liked a

14
00:00:28,583 --> 00:00:30,482
photo minutes after posting it.

15
00:00:31,132 --> 00:00:33,339
We replay a single awkward comment for

16
00:00:33,339 --> 00:00:33,770
days.

17
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:37,232
We change our clothes, our words, even our

18
00:00:37,232 --> 00:00:38,732
opinions — to fit in.

19
00:00:39,382 --> 00:00:42,192
Why? Why does a creature that loves itself

20
00:00:42,192 --> 00:00:44,751
so much need other people to tell it

21
00:00:44,751 --> 00:00:45,875
that it's okay?

22
00:00:46,525 --> 00:00:48,621
By the end of this video, you'll know what

23
00:00:48,621 --> 00:00:50,530
happens in your brain when you're left

24
00:00:50,530 --> 00:00:50,857
out,

25
00:00:51,507 --> 00:00:53,465
why one of history's most famous

26
00:00:53,465 --> 00:00:55,760
experiments made people deny what they

27
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:57,607
could see with their own eyes,

28
00:00:58,257 --> 00:01:00,737
how social media turned approval into a

29
00:01:00,737 --> 00:01:01,741
slot machine —

30
00:01:02,391 --> 00:01:04,324
and what Nietzsche thought we should do

31
00:01:04,324 --> 00:01:05,159
about all of it.

32
00:01:05,809 --> 00:01:07,613
Quick favor before we start:

33
00:01:07,613 --> 00:01:10,057
if this question hits a little close to

34
00:01:10,057 --> 00:01:11,279
home, subscribe.

35
00:01:11,279 --> 00:01:13,257
It genuinely helps the channel.

36
00:01:13,907 --> 00:01:16,001
Let's start with a simple idea:

37
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:18,586
the need for approval isn't a character

38
00:01:18,586 --> 00:01:20,064
flaw. It's ancient.

39
00:01:20,714 --> 00:01:23,144
For most of human history, we lived in

40
00:01:23,144 --> 00:01:25,811
small groups — often estimated at around a

41
00:01:25,811 --> 00:01:27,471
hundred and fifty people.

42
00:01:28,121 --> 00:01:30,513
Food, protection, a partner, care when you

43
00:01:30,513 --> 00:01:32,852
were sick: all of it came from the group.

44
00:01:33,502 --> 00:01:35,740
Being pushed out wasn't just lonely.

45
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:37,117
It could be deadly.

46
00:01:37,117 --> 00:01:39,585
A human alone on the savanna didn't last

47
00:01:39,585 --> 00:01:40,044
long.

48
00:01:40,694 --> 00:01:43,241
So evolution gave us an alarm system.

49
00:01:43,241 --> 00:01:45,724
An inner voice that constantly asks:

50
00:01:45,724 --> 00:01:47,252
Am I still accepted?

51
00:01:47,252 --> 00:01:48,398
Am I still in?

52
00:01:49,048 --> 00:01:51,868
In 1995, psychologists Roy Baumeister

53
00:01:51,868 --> 00:01:54,488
and Mark Leary reviewed decades of

54
00:01:54,488 --> 00:01:57,174
research and argued that the need to

55
00:01:57,174 --> 00:02:00,129
belong is a fundamental human motivation

56
00:02:00,129 --> 00:02:00,398
—

57
00:02:01,048 --> 00:02:03,240
almost as basic as hunger.

58
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:05,206
Not a luxury. A need.

59
00:02:05,856 --> 00:02:08,068
And that alarm system is surprisingly

60
00:02:08,068 --> 00:02:08,732
physical.

61
00:02:09,382 --> 00:02:11,943
In 2003, researchers led by Naomi

62
00:02:11,943 --> 00:02:15,057
Eisenberger put people in a brain scanner

63
00:02:15,057 --> 00:02:17,548
and had them play a simple online

64
00:02:17,548 --> 00:02:20,317
ball-tossing game called Cyberball.

65
00:02:20,967 --> 00:02:22,688
Three players pass a ball.

66
00:02:22,688 --> 00:02:25,299
After a while, the other two stop passing

67
00:02:25,299 --> 00:02:26,723
it to you. That's it.

68
00:02:27,373 --> 00:02:29,866
They weren't real people — just a computer

69
00:02:29,866 --> 00:02:30,476
program.

70
00:02:31,126 --> 00:02:33,691
And yet being left out activated parts of

71
00:02:33,691 --> 00:02:36,140
the brain that are also involved in the

72
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:37,947
distress of physical pain.

73
00:02:38,597 --> 00:02:41,334
Scientists still debate exactly how close

74
00:02:41,334 --> 00:02:44,070
social pain and physical pain really are.

75
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:47,398
But the basic finding has been repeated

76
00:02:47,398 --> 00:02:49,821
many times: being excluded, even by

77
00:02:49,821 --> 00:02:52,435
strangers, even in a meaningless game,

78
00:02:52,435 --> 00:02:53,137
hurts.

79
00:02:53,787 --> 00:02:56,478
Mark Leary also proposed what he called

80
00:02:56,478 --> 00:02:58,208
the sociometer theory:

81
00:02:58,208 --> 00:03:00,835
the idea that self-esteem works like a

82
00:03:00,835 --> 00:03:01,411
gauge.

83
00:03:02,061 --> 00:03:04,146
It isn't really measuring how good you

84
00:03:04,146 --> 00:03:06,130
are. It's measuring how accepted you

85
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:06,638
feel.

86
00:03:07,288 --> 00:03:09,552
When the needle drops, you feel worthless

87
00:03:09,552 --> 00:03:11,765
— even if nothing about you has changed.

88
00:03:12,415 --> 00:03:15,078
If rejection hurts this much, how far will

89
00:03:15,078 --> 00:03:16,320
we go to avoid it?

90
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:20,432
In the 1950s, psychologist Solomon Asch

91
00:03:20,432 --> 00:03:22,556
ran a famous experiment.

92
00:03:23,206 --> 00:03:25,485
A person sits in a room with several

93
00:03:25,485 --> 00:03:27,882
others and is shown a line, then three

94
00:03:27,882 --> 00:03:30,104
more lines. The question is simple:

95
00:03:30,104 --> 00:03:32,500
which of these three matches the first

96
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:32,910
one?

97
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:34,971
The answer is obvious.

98
00:03:35,621 --> 00:03:37,982
But everyone else in the room is secretly

99
00:03:37,982 --> 00:03:40,396
working with the experimenter — and one by

100
00:03:40,396 --> 00:03:42,596
one, they confidently give the wrong

101
00:03:42,596 --> 00:03:43,132
answer.

102
00:03:43,782 --> 00:03:44,914
Then it's your turn.

103
00:03:45,564 --> 00:03:47,941
Across the critical trials, people went

104
00:03:47,941 --> 00:03:50,261
along with the group's obviously wrong

105
00:03:50,261 --> 00:03:52,298
answer about a third of the time.

106
00:03:52,948 --> 00:03:55,452
And around three out of four participants

107
00:03:55,452 --> 00:03:56,989
conformed at least once.

108
00:03:57,639 --> 00:03:59,784
Many later said they knew the answer was

109
00:03:59,784 --> 00:04:02,030
wrong. They just didn't want to be the one

110
00:04:02,030 --> 00:04:02,879
who stood out.

111
00:04:03,529 --> 00:04:05,835
When Asch gave them a single ally — one

112
00:04:05,835 --> 00:04:08,031
other person who answered correctly —

113
00:04:08,031 --> 00:04:09,678
conformity dropped sharply.

114
00:04:10,328 --> 00:04:12,896
Sometimes we don't need everyone to agree

115
00:04:12,896 --> 00:04:14,706
with us. We just need one.

116
00:04:15,356 --> 00:04:17,676
There's one more trap worth knowing about.

117
00:04:17,676 --> 00:04:19,687
Psychologists call it the spotlight

118
00:04:19,687 --> 00:04:20,254
effect.

119
00:04:20,904 --> 00:04:23,403
In a 2000 study, Thomas Gilovich and

120
00:04:23,403 --> 00:04:26,084
colleagues asked college students to walk

121
00:04:26,084 --> 00:04:28,827
into a room wearing a T-shirt with a large

122
00:04:28,827 --> 00:04:30,716
picture of Barry Manilow —

123
00:04:31,366 --> 00:04:33,068
a singer the students considered

124
00:04:33,068 --> 00:04:34,235
embarrassing to wear.

125
00:04:34,885 --> 00:04:37,014
The students guessed that about half the

126
00:04:37,014 --> 00:04:39,144
people in the room would notice the shirt.

127
00:04:39,794 --> 00:04:42,108
In reality, only about a quarter did.

128
00:04:42,758 --> 00:04:44,657
We feel like we're standing under a

129
00:04:44,657 --> 00:04:46,805
spotlight. But everyone else is standing

130
00:04:46,805 --> 00:04:49,053
under their own — busy wondering what we

131
00:04:49,053 --> 00:04:50,002
think of them.

132
00:04:50,652 --> 00:04:52,862
Approval isn't only about being in the

133
00:04:52,862 --> 00:04:54,969
group. It's also about where you stand

134
00:04:54,969 --> 00:04:55,740
inside it.

135
00:04:56,390 --> 00:04:59,063
In 1967, researchers began following

136
00:04:59,063 --> 00:05:01,872
thousands of British civil servants in

137
00:05:01,872 --> 00:05:04,477
what became known as the Whitehall

138
00:05:04,477 --> 00:05:05,299
studies.

139
00:05:05,949 --> 00:05:08,497
These were all office workers, with jobs,

140
00:05:08,497 --> 00:05:10,697
salaries, and access to healthcare.

141
00:05:11,347 --> 00:05:13,803
And yet, the lower someone's grade in the

142
00:05:13,803 --> 00:05:16,204
hierarchy, the worse their health tended

143
00:05:16,204 --> 00:05:18,549
to be — including higher rates of heart

144
00:05:18,549 --> 00:05:20,168
disease and earlier death.

145
00:05:20,818 --> 00:05:23,237
Money and habits explained part of it, but

146
00:05:23,237 --> 00:05:24,150
not all of it.

147
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,259
One idea the researchers emphasized:

148
00:05:27,259 --> 00:05:29,907
lower status often means less control

149
00:05:29,907 --> 00:05:32,428
over your own life — and that kind of

150
00:05:32,428 --> 00:05:34,824
chronic stress wears the body down.

151
00:05:35,474 --> 00:05:38,410
In 1954, psychologist Leon Festinger

152
00:05:38,410 --> 00:05:40,058
added another piece.

153
00:05:40,058 --> 00:05:42,922
In his social comparison theory, he

154
00:05:42,922 --> 00:05:45,930
argued that we often judge ourselves by

155
00:05:45,930 --> 00:05:48,294
comparing ourselves to others.

156
00:05:48,944 --> 00:05:51,100
Not "Am I doing well?" — but "Am I doing

157
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:52,505
well compared to them?"

158
00:05:53,155 --> 00:05:55,475
For most of history, "them" meant a few

159
00:05:55,475 --> 00:05:57,850
dozen neighbors. Then something changed.

160
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:00,972
Social media took that ancient approval

161
00:06:00,972 --> 00:06:03,503
system and connected it to billions of

162
00:06:03,503 --> 00:06:05,504
people — with numbers attached.

163
00:06:06,154 --> 00:06:09,021
In 2016, a team led by Lauren Sherman

164
00:06:09,021 --> 00:06:12,093
scanned the brains of teenagers while they

165
00:06:12,093 --> 00:06:14,823
looked at photos on an Instagram-like

166
00:06:14,823 --> 00:06:15,369
feed.

167
00:06:16,019 --> 00:06:18,606
When a photo appeared to have many likes,

168
00:06:18,606 --> 00:06:21,192
a region linked to reward — the nucleus

169
00:06:21,192 --> 00:06:23,190
accumbens — became more active.

170
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:26,409
The teens were also more likely to like a

171
00:06:26,409 --> 00:06:28,979
photo themselves when it already had lots

172
00:06:28,979 --> 00:06:29,679
of likes.

173
00:06:30,329 --> 00:06:32,338
And the rewards are unpredictable.

174
00:06:32,338 --> 00:06:34,671
Sometimes a post does well, sometimes it

175
00:06:34,671 --> 00:06:35,268
doesn't.

176
00:06:35,918 --> 00:06:38,321
Decades ago, the psychologist B.

177
00:06:38,321 --> 00:06:41,257
F. Skinner showed that rewards delivered

178
00:06:41,257 --> 00:06:44,193
on an unpredictable schedule produce some

179
00:06:44,193 --> 00:06:46,996
of the most persistent behavior of all.

180
00:06:47,646 --> 00:06:50,015
It's the same principle that keeps people

181
00:06:50,015 --> 00:06:52,115
pulling the lever of a slot machine.

182
00:06:52,765 --> 00:06:55,499
In 2017, Facebook's founding president,

183
00:06:55,499 --> 00:06:58,299
Sean Parker, described the platform as

184
00:06:58,299 --> 00:07:01,034
"a social-validation feedback loop" —

185
00:07:01,684 --> 00:07:04,242
and said it exploited "a vulnerability in

186
00:07:04,242 --> 00:07:05,493
human psychology."

187
00:07:06,143 --> 00:07:08,590
And now the comparison isn't with a few

188
00:07:08,590 --> 00:07:11,096
neighbors. It's with the best moments of

189
00:07:11,096 --> 00:07:13,602
thousands of strangers, carefully chosen

190
00:07:13,602 --> 00:07:14,534
and filtered.

191
00:07:15,184 --> 00:07:18,079
In 2019, Instagram began testing hiding

192
00:07:18,079 --> 00:07:20,561
like counts in several countries.

193
00:07:20,561 --> 00:07:23,595
Later, it let users choose whether to see

194
00:07:23,595 --> 00:07:24,629
them at all.

195
00:07:25,279 --> 00:07:27,250
Even the platforms seem to know the

196
00:07:27,250 --> 00:07:28,807
numbers do something to us.

197
00:07:29,457 --> 00:07:32,228
More than a century before social media, a

198
00:07:32,228 --> 00:07:34,753
German philosopher was already worried

199
00:07:34,753 --> 00:07:36,046
about all of this.

200
00:07:36,696 --> 00:07:38,956
Friedrich Nietzsche believed that much

201
00:07:38,956 --> 00:07:41,268
of what we call morality grew out of what

202
00:07:41,268 --> 00:07:43,054
he called the herd instinct —

203
00:07:43,704 --> 00:07:46,368
the deep urge to obey, to fit in, to value

204
00:07:46,368 --> 00:07:48,084
whatever the group values.

205
00:07:48,734 --> 00:07:51,788
In Beyond Good and Evil, published in

206
00:07:51,788 --> 00:07:54,770
1886, he argued that this instinct for

207
00:07:54,770 --> 00:07:58,042
obedience had been trained into people for

208
00:07:58,042 --> 00:07:59,642
thousands of years.

209
00:08:00,292 --> 00:08:02,806
In Thus Spoke Zarathustra, he imagined

210
00:08:02,806 --> 00:08:04,093
where it all leads:

211
00:08:04,093 --> 00:08:06,256
a figure he called the last man.

212
00:08:06,256 --> 00:08:08,829
Comfortable, cautious, afraid of standing

213
00:08:08,829 --> 00:08:09,239
out.

214
00:08:09,889 --> 00:08:12,260
Zarathustra describes this world in one

215
00:08:12,260 --> 00:08:13,220
chilling line:

216
00:08:13,870 --> 00:08:15,547
"No shepherd and one herd!

217
00:08:15,547 --> 00:08:17,951
Everyone wants the same, everyone is the

218
00:08:17,951 --> 00:08:18,454
same;

219
00:08:19,104 --> 00:08:20,967
whoever feels differently goes

220
00:08:20,967 --> 00:08:22,884
voluntarily into the madhouse."

221
00:08:23,534 --> 00:08:25,814
For Nietzsche, the danger of living for

222
00:08:25,814 --> 00:08:28,040
approval wasn't just that it makes you

223
00:08:28,040 --> 00:08:30,375
anxious. It's that it makes you small.

224
00:08:31,025 --> 00:08:33,674
You end up living a life designed by other

225
00:08:33,674 --> 00:08:34,911
people's opinions.

226
00:08:35,561 --> 00:08:37,416
So what did Nietzsche want instead?

227
00:08:38,066 --> 00:08:40,344
In The Gay Science, he wrote a short

228
00:08:40,344 --> 00:08:42,733
line that became one of his most famous:

229
00:08:42,733 --> 00:08:44,677
"What does your conscience say?

230
00:08:44,677 --> 00:08:47,066
— You shall become the person you are."

231
00:08:47,716 --> 00:08:50,321
The idea isn't to stop caring about other

232
00:08:50,321 --> 00:08:52,925
people. It's to stop handing them the job

233
00:08:52,925 --> 00:08:55,233
of deciding what your life is worth.

234
00:08:55,883 --> 00:08:58,297
To create your own values — and live by

235
00:08:58,297 --> 00:09:00,480
them, even when no one is clapping.

236
00:09:01,130 --> 00:09:02,438
And here's the twist:

237
00:09:02,438 --> 00:09:04,727
Nietzsche lived that way, and it wasn't

238
00:09:04,727 --> 00:09:06,689
glamorous. His books barely sold.

239
00:09:07,339 --> 00:09:09,393
The fourth part of Thus Spoke

240
00:09:09,393 --> 00:09:11,758
Zarathustra was printed at his own

241
00:09:11,758 --> 00:09:14,497
expense, in fewer than fifty copies, most

242
00:09:14,497 --> 00:09:16,925
of which he kept or gave to friends.

243
00:09:17,575 --> 00:09:20,180
Near the end of his working life, he wrote

244
00:09:20,180 --> 00:09:22,439
an autobiography called Ecce Homo,

245
00:09:23,089 --> 00:09:25,355
with chapter titles like "Why I Am So

246
00:09:25,355 --> 00:09:27,400
Wise" and "Why I Write Such Good

247
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:28,008
Books."

248
00:09:28,658 --> 00:09:30,124
Maybe that was irony.

249
00:09:30,124 --> 00:09:31,775
Maybe it was confidence.

250
00:09:32,425 --> 00:09:34,551
Or maybe it was a man who had stopped

251
00:09:34,551 --> 00:09:36,731
waiting for the world's approval — and

252
00:09:36,731 --> 00:09:38,485
decided to give it to himself.

253
00:09:39,135 --> 00:09:41,054
Psychology has its own version of

254
00:09:41,054 --> 00:09:42,280
Nietzsche's warning.

255
00:09:42,930 --> 00:09:45,284
The psychologist Carl Rogers noticed

256
00:09:45,284 --> 00:09:47,868
that many of his clients had grown up with

257
00:09:47,868 --> 00:09:50,165
what he called conditions of worth:

258
00:09:50,815 --> 00:09:53,161
the feeling that they were lovable only

259
00:09:53,161 --> 00:09:55,397
if they were good, successful, quiet,

260
00:09:55,397 --> 00:09:57,743
impressive — whatever earned approval at

261
00:09:57,743 --> 00:09:58,180
home.

262
00:09:58,830 --> 00:10:01,180
As adults, they carried those conditions

263
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:03,422
inside them. Every rejection felt like

264
00:10:03,422 --> 00:10:05,280
proof they had failed the test.

265
00:10:05,930 --> 00:10:08,548
Rogers believed that what helps people

266
00:10:08,548 --> 00:10:11,357
heal is unconditional positive regard —

267
00:10:11,357 --> 00:10:14,230
being accepted as a person, even when your

268
00:10:14,230 --> 00:10:16,146
behavior isn't approved of.

269
00:10:16,796 --> 00:10:19,514
Once people feel that basic acceptance, he

270
00:10:19,514 --> 00:10:21,749
observed, they often become less

271
00:10:21,749 --> 00:10:24,467
dependent on everyone else's approval, not

272
00:10:24,467 --> 00:10:24,951
more.

273
00:10:25,601 --> 00:10:26,919
Let's try something.

274
00:10:26,919 --> 00:10:29,498
Think about the last time you changed what

275
00:10:29,498 --> 00:10:31,790
you were going to say, because of how

276
00:10:31,790 --> 00:10:33,051
people might react.

277
00:10:33,701 --> 00:10:35,636
Now ask yourself three questions.

278
00:10:36,286 --> 00:10:39,317
First: whose approval was I looking for?

279
00:10:39,317 --> 00:10:41,607
Not "people" — a specific face.

280
00:10:42,257 --> 00:10:44,662
Second: if that person disapproved, what

281
00:10:44,662 --> 00:10:46,060
would I actually lose?

282
00:10:46,710 --> 00:10:49,271
Be honest. Sometimes the answer is "a

283
00:10:49,271 --> 00:10:51,705
lot." Often, it's "almost nothing."

284
00:10:52,355 --> 00:10:54,307
Third: if no one would ever see it, what

285
00:10:54,307 --> 00:10:55,261
would I have done?

286
00:10:55,911 --> 00:10:58,357
That last answer is a small window into

287
00:10:58,357 --> 00:11:00,862
the person Nietzsche told you to become.

288
00:11:01,512 --> 00:11:03,252
So should we stop caring what others

289
00:11:03,252 --> 00:11:03,653
think?

290
00:11:04,303 --> 00:11:07,185
Not quite. In 2010, a large review of

291
00:11:07,185 --> 00:11:10,067
nearly a hundred and fifty studies by

292
00:11:10,067 --> 00:11:13,021
Julianne Holt-Lunstad and colleagues

293
00:11:13,671 --> 00:11:16,081
found that people with stronger social

294
00:11:16,081 --> 00:11:18,550
relationships had about a fifty percent

295
00:11:18,550 --> 00:11:21,019
greater likelihood of survival over the

296
00:11:21,019 --> 00:11:22,018
study periods.

297
00:11:22,668 --> 00:11:24,306
We are built for connection.

298
00:11:24,306 --> 00:11:26,155
The goal isn't to need no one.

299
00:11:26,805 --> 00:11:29,141
The difference is between belonging and

300
00:11:29,141 --> 00:11:31,212
approval. Approval is conditional:

301
00:11:31,212 --> 00:11:32,858
you get it when you perform.

302
00:11:32,858 --> 00:11:35,088
Belonging is being accepted as you are.

303
00:11:35,738 --> 00:11:37,768
Chasing approval can actually push

304
00:11:37,768 --> 00:11:40,073
belonging further away — because people

305
00:11:40,073 --> 00:11:42,432
can't connect with someone who is always

306
00:11:42,432 --> 00:11:43,420
wearing a mask.

307
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:46,466
Marcus Aurelius was right to be puzzled.

308
00:11:46,466 --> 00:11:48,862
We love ourselves more than anyone — and

309
00:11:48,862 --> 00:11:51,091
still let strangers decide what we're

310
00:11:51,091 --> 00:11:51,592
worth.

311
00:11:52,242 --> 00:11:54,674
Maybe the answer isn't to silence that

312
00:11:54,674 --> 00:11:56,988
ancient alarm. It kept our ancestors

313
00:11:56,988 --> 00:11:57,522
alive.

314
00:11:58,172 --> 00:12:00,511
Maybe the answer is to notice when it's

315
00:12:00,511 --> 00:12:02,292
ringing — and to ask, calmly:

316
00:12:02,292 --> 00:12:03,852
Is this really a threat?

317
00:12:03,852 --> 00:12:05,244
Or is it just a like?

318
00:12:05,894 --> 00:12:08,195
Because in the end, the opinion you'll

319
00:12:08,195 --> 00:12:09,710
live with the longest...

320
00:12:09,710 --> 00:12:10,552
is your own.

